  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a thorough explanation of why \"pure\" gold is 99.9% and not 100%:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2c The Science: 100% Pure Gold Is Physically Impossible\n\nPerfect purity would mean a piece of gold has zero non-gold atoms. But nature has its own plans \u2014 pure gold naturally has some other atoms mingling within it, and these so-called \"impurities\" actually have a job: they help stabilize the metal's structure.\n\nIn truth, it is impossible to produce 100% pure gold based on current technical limits. To truly achieve 100% purity, every single atom in the sample would need to be a gold atom \u2014 including ensuring no gold radioisotopes are present \u2014 which is practically unachievable.\n\n---\n\n### \u2699\ufe0f The Refining Process Has Limits\n\nRefining metals to remove impurities costs money, and there's no benefit in making gold bullion used for monetary or jewelry purposes any better than 99.99% \u2014 doing so just raises cost with no practical benefit.\n\nIt is currently impossible to refine gold to a purity of 100% (1,000 fineness). 999.999 fineness is the purest gold ever produced, and proved too expensive to be a regular occurrence.\n\nGenerally, the closest to 100% pure gold that refiners are able to reach in everyday production is a fineness of 999.9 gold.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccf How Purity Is Measured\n\nGold's purity is graded based on the **millesimal fineness scale**, which is used to determine the purity of a precious metal by measuring the number of parts per thousand of pure metal. Typically, anything that is 99.9% gold (999) or more is considered fine gold.\n\nThe purest gold is 999.99, also called **\"five nines\" fine**. Just below this is 999.9 gold, which is also called **\"four nines\" fine**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc5 Industry Standards\n\nAnything at or above 999 gold is still considered pure and would fetch the same price when selling to a dealer. The British Royal Mint, the Royal Canadian Mint, and the Perth Mint all strike their gold coins to a purity of 999.9 gold, making it one of the most commonly seen purities for bullion coins and bars.\n\n---\n\n### In Summary\n\nThe 99.9% (or 999) figure is not a shortcoming of the gold industry \u2014 it reflects a **fundamental physical and technical reality**. Trace amounts of other atoms are naturally present in gold and nearly impossible to fully eliminate. The \"99.9%\" label is simply an honest reflection of the highest level of purity achievable under practical and economical conditions.",
